#!/usr/bin/perl
use v5.36;

use Specman::CLI;

exit Specman::CLI->new(pod => __FILE__)->run(@ARGV);

=head1 NAME

specman - Manage project requirements

=head1 SYNOPSIS

	specman [--dir DIR] list [--status STATUS] [--tag TAG] [--json]
	specman [--dir DIR] show ID [--json]
	specman [--dir DIR] validate [--json]
	specman --help | --version

=head1 DESCRIPTION

B<specman> works with a project's requirements, stored as one file per
requirement in a directory, in the format described in
L<Specman::Manual::Format>.

Text output is meant for people. For use by other tools, every command
accepts B<--json>, which prints a single line of JSON instead.

=head1 COMMANDS

=head2 list

Lists the requirements, one per line, with their identifier, status, priority
and title:

	ID       STATUS    PRIORITY  TITLE
	REQ-001  approved  high      Manage project requirements
	REQ-002  approved  high      Store requirements as text files

With B<--json>, prints an array of objects, one per requirement, holding
every field except C<body>, and C<file>, the requirement's file name.

=head2 show I<ID>

Shows one requirement: its fields, the requirements related to it (as
I<refined by>, I<needed by> and I<conflicts with>), and its body:

	id:          REQ-002
	title:       Store requirements as text files
	status:      approved
	priority:    high
	author:      Maurolepis Dreki
	created:     2026-10-27
	tags:        storage
	refines:     REQ-001 (Manage project requirements)
	refined by:  REQ-003, REQ-004

	Each requirement shall be stored as one text file, kept under version control
	with git.

With B<--json>, prints an object holding every field, C<file>, and
C<incoming>: for each relation type, the identifiers of the requirements with
that relation to this one.

=head2 validate

Checks every requirement and the relations between them, and prints each
problem found, one per line, as in C<REQ-002.md: tags: item 2: is empty>.
Warnings are prefixed with C<warning:>; they are worth attention, but do not
make the requirements invalid. A summary is printed to standard error:

	specman: 8 requirements, 0 errors

The checks cover:

=over

=item *

each file: valid UTF-8, LF line endings, a header that is a valid YAML
mapping, and a file name matching the requirement's C<id>;

=item *

each requirement: required fields present, no unknown fields, and every value
in the right form; no relation to itself; a body unless it is a draft;

=item *

all requirements together: unique identifiers, relations to requirements that
exist, and no cycles in C<refines> or C<depends_on>.

=back

With B<--json>, prints an object holding C<valid>, true or false, and
C<errors>, an array of objects, one per problem:

	{"errors":[{"field":"status","file":"REQ-001.md","id":"REQ-001","message":"'done' is not one of draft, approved, implemented, obsolete","severity":"error","text":"REQ-001.md: status: 'done' is not one of draft, approved, implemented, obsolete"}],"valid":false}

Each object holds:

=over

=item C<message>

The problem, without its location.

=item C<severity>

C<error> or C<warning>.

=item C<text>

The problem as printed without B<--json>.

=item C<file>, C<id>, C<field>, C<item>

Where the problem is; only the parts that apply are present. C<item> counts
from 1.

=back

=head1 OPTIONS

=over

=item B<-d>, B<--dir> I<DIR>

The directory holding the requirement files. Defaults to F<requirements> in
the current directory.

=item B<--status> I<STATUS>

With B<list>, lists only requirements with the given status.

=item B<--tag> I<TAG>

With B<list>, lists only requirements with the given tag.

=item B<--json>

Prints JSON instead of text.

=item B<-h>, B<--help>

Prints this help and exits.

=item B<-V>, B<--version>

Prints the version and exits.

=back

=head1 EXIT STATUS

=over

=item B<0>

Success. For B<validate>: no errors were found.

=item B<1>

For B<validate>: errors were found; warnings alone do not count. For B<show>: no requirement has the
given identifier.

=item B<2>

The command line or the requirements directory is invalid.

=back

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<Specman>, L<Specman::Manual::Format>

